  "problem": "$(f∘h)(4)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The expression $(f∘h)(4)$ is equivalent to $f(h(4)).$\n\nPreviously, we found that \n\n$$\nh(4)=9.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=9$ into $f(x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h(4)) & =f(9) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{9}+2 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3+2 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
